# Force LF for files that EXECUTE on Linux (the EC2 box). The dev machine is Windows
# with core.autocrlf=true, which makes `git archive` inject CRLF into shell scripts and
# systemd units → `$'\r': command not found` / `set: pipefail<CR>: invalid option` at
# runtime on the box. eol=lf pins LF in the working tree AND in `git archive` output,
# independent of the local autocrlf setting. (Found 2026-06-30 during the CloudWatch
# publisher deploy; protects the ADR-0032 cutover scripts too.)
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer   text eol=lf

# ======================================================================================
# DEPLOY-EOL DETERMINISM (2026-08-22) -- the runtime source must BE the Git bytes.
#
# The paper box has no .git: code arrives as a `git archive` tarball extracted into
# /opt/workbench/app. `git archive` applies EOL conversion, so on a workstation with
# core.autocrlf=true it emits CRLF for every file Git treats as text that carries no eol
# attribute. Measured on this tree immediately before this block was added: 2,078 of 2,239
# archived files (93%) did not match their Git blob -- 1,225 .py, 508 .md, 117 .json,
# 114 .tsx. Only the three pins above and the -text protections below were safe.
#
# That is how the 2026-08-21 22:57Z redeploy shipped app/research/disc_mdq/ledger.py with
# 670 CR bytes. Nothing broke -- Python reads CRLF, and the governed-artifact loader
# LF-normalizes before comparing pins -- but "the deployed bytes ARE the reviewed bytes"
# stopped being true, and every deployment hash claim needed an "up to EOL conversion"
# caveat. The property this block establishes is:
#
#     Git blob -> deterministic archive -> extracted runtime source == exact Git bytes
#
# NOT: Git blob -> workstation checkout rules -> maybe CRLF -> normalize later and declare
# it equivalent. Normalized hashing stays correct for governed DATA semantics; it is not a
# substitute for exact source-deployment identity.
#
# Enforced by scripts/check_deploy_archive_determinism.py, which builds the archive under
# BOTH core.autocrlf settings and fails on any byte mismatch against the blobs. Add a new
# text file type and that check fails until it is pinned here -- the cheap failure.
#
# ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING: the LAST matching rule wins, so every `-text` protection below
# (digest-pinned manifests, the drift-audit tree, and any future one) MUST stay AFTER this
# block. Appending a generic `*.json text eol=lf` at the end of this file would silently
# override `manifests/layer2/*.json -text` and rewrite digest-pinned bytes.
#
# Binaries are deliberately NOT force-converted. Git's own binary detection protects
# undeclared binary content, and the `-text` rules protect the digest-pinned trees. This
# block names text extensions ONLY -- never replace it with a blanket `* text=auto`.
# ======================================================================================
*.py      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.ts      text eol=lf
*.tsx     text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.ini     text eol=lf
*.cfg     text eol=lf
*.csv     text eol=lf
*.sql     text eol=lf
*.html    text eol=lf
*.css     text eol=lf
*.sha256  text eol=lf
*.mako    text eol=lf
*.mdc     text eol=lf
*.pine    text eol=lf
*.ps1     text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf
*.conf    text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitkeep        text eol=lf

# Windows-only launchers, never executed on the Linux box. cmd.exe has historically been
# fragile with LF-only batch files (labels / goto), so pin them CRLF: the archive stays
# DETERMINISTIC -- the property that matters -- while keeping the form Windows expects.
# They are the single documented exception to archive-equals-blob, and the checker carries
# them in an explicit allowlist rather than silently skipping them.
*.bat     text eol=crlf

docs/review/momentum_daily/drift_audit/** -text

# The Layer 2 governed artifacts are pinned BY DIGEST in corpus_manifest_v2.json. Any
# end-of-line translation changes their bytes and therefore their identity, which the
# runtime refuses. Never let git rewrite them.
manifests/layer2/*.json -text

apps/backend/app/altdata/sec001_v3/** -text
apps/backend/tests/altdata/sec001_v3/** -text
